## Scenario 1: Thundering Herd (the concurrent agent problem)

Signal: 100 agents start at the same time, all hitting the same LLM provider. Provider returns 429 (rate limit). Agents retry with exponential backoff. Queue depth explodes.
Cause: A batch job kicked off 100 concurrent workflows, or a marketing campaign drove 100 users to hit "analyze my data" at the same time.

Let us decompose the thundering herd into its distinct failure mechanisms, each with the problem, the root cause, and the solution — then tie them into one control plane. The key realization up front:

--- The bottleneck is the provider's quota, not your workers. Everything downstream is a consequence of contending for a shared, rate-limited resource. Most teams scale the wrong thing and make it worse.

The physics you must internalize first
A long-horizon agent is not one request. It's a stream of LLM calls over time — dozens to hundreds of calls, each large (long context), each consuming two quotas: RPM (requests/min) and TPM (tokens/min). So your aggregate demand is:

demand = active_users × agents_per_user × calls_per_agent × tokens_per_call

For long-context agents, TPM is almost always the binding constraint, not RPM — a single 100K-token call can consume more TPM than a hundred small calls. And the provider's quota is fixed while your demand is bursty. That mismatch is the entire problem.

S1 — The synchronized burst (100 agents start at once)
Problem: 100 agents begin simultaneously; their first LLM calls land on the provider in the same second.
Root cause: Bursts are rarely organic. They're self-inflicted or event-driven:
Cron alignment — every batch job scheduled at 0 * * * * fires at the top of the hour.
Batch jobs — a nightly ETL kicks off 100 workflows at once.
Marketing / news events — a campaign or headline drives a traffic spike.
Retry synchronization — (see S3) a prior 429 wave reschedules everyone at the same instant.
Solution:
Admission control at the door: cap concurrent agent starts with a semaphore sized to your provider quota. New starts beyond the cap queue, they don't fail.
Smoothing (leaky bucket): release agent starts at a steady rate rather than in a spike.
Stagger cron: never schedule at the top of the hour. Add per-job jitter (0 * * * * → 7,37 * * * * or a random offset). This alone removes a huge class of self-inflicted bursts.
Deadline-aware admission: if an agent's SLO can't be met given the current queue, reject at the door with a Retry-After rather than admit and miss the SLO.
If you get it wrong: You admit everything, the provider 429s the whole wave, and you've turned one burst into a retry storm (S3) plus a queue explosion (S4).

S2 — The provider 429 wall (RPM/TPM exhaustion)
Problem: The provider returns 429 (rate limit) or 529/503 (overloaded) because aggregate demand exceeds quota.
Root cause: Your quota is shared across all tenants and all agents, and you're consuming it faster than it refills. Because TPM is the binding constraint for long-context agents, a few large agents can exhaust it even when RPM looks fine.
Solution:
Treat quota as a first-class resource with a provider-quota scheduler. Maintain a virtual queue per provider key: before an agent issues an LLM call, it must acquire a slot (RPM) and reserve tokens (TPM) against the key's budget. If the budget is full, the call waits or routes elsewhere — you convert a 429 into a routing decision. This is the same reserve/settle pattern we used for money and KV cache.
Multi-key / multi-provider spread: distribute load across several API keys, regions, and providers. A 429 on one key routes to another.
Fallback models: on sustained 429, degrade to a cheaper/secondary model or a self-hosted model rather than failing.
Track consumption in real time: a Redis token bucket per key, decremented on issue, trued-up on the actual usage returned.
If you get it wrong: You discover the quota only by receiving 429s — i.e., you learn your limit by crashing into it, and every 429 is a user-facing failure.


S3 — The retry storm (amplification)
Problem: 100 agents get 429s and retry with exponential backoff. The retry traffic adds to the load. Queue depth explodes. The system gets worse the harder it tries to recover.
Root cause: Retries are themselves load, and they're uncoordinated. Each agent retries independently with no global view, so:
Without jitter, backoff synchronizes (everyone retries at t+1s, t+2s, t+4s) → repeated waves.
With jitter it's better, but N agents × M retries still multiplies the request rate.
A retry storm can exceed the original load — the classic congestion collapse.
Solution:
Full jitter: sleep = random(0, min(cap, base * 2^attempt)). Desynchronizes retries.
Respect Retry-After: the provider tells you when to retry; honor it instead of your own guess.
Retry budget: cap retries per workflow (e.g., 3) AND a global retry budget (retries ≤ 20% of traffic). When the global budget is exhausted, stop retrying and shed instead.
Circuit breaker per provider: if the 429 rate crosses a threshold, open the breaker and fail fast for a window instead of hammering a provider that can't serve you. Half-open probes test recovery.
Distinguish retryable from non-retryable: 429/503/timeout retry; 400/401 fail immediately. Retrying a 400 wastes quota.
Load shedding over retrying: when overloaded, it's better to defer or drop low-priority work than to retry everything.
If you get it wrong: The retry storm outlives the original burst. You keep hammering a provider that's already saturated, and your own queue fills with retry tasks.


S4 — The queue explosion & blocked workers (backpressure)
Problem: Tasks pile up waiting on LLM calls that keep failing. Queue depth explodes; latency is unbounded; workers are occupied but not making progress.
Root cause: Each in-flight LLM call holds a worker thread/connection. When the provider is slow or failing, workers are blocked, not working — they occupy capacity while producing nothing. The queue grows because arrivals exceed completions, and completions have stalled.
Solution:
Async, non-blocking IO: a worker holds many in-flight calls (event loop), so a slow provider doesn't pin one-thread-per-call.
Bounded queue with shedding at the door: reject new work with 429 + Retry-After when the queue crosses a threshold. An unbounded queue is a lie — it promises work you can't deliver.
Deadline-aware shedding: if queue-wait + expected-runtime > the task's deadline, reject at the door rather than waste GPU/provider quota on work that will be late.
Backpressure propagation: signal upstream (the gateway, the batch scheduler) to slow down, so the pressure is felt at the source, not absorbed in the middle.
If you get it wrong: You build an ever-growing queue that eventually OOMs or times out en masse — a cliff instead of a graceful slope.


S5 — The lost long-horizon work (the 90%-done agent)
Problem: An agent that has made 50 LLM calls and is 90% done hits a 429. If it fails, you lose all 50 calls of work and the tokens you spent. If it retries forever, it holds resources.
Root cause: Long-horizon agents have large sunk cost. A naive retry-from-scratch model destroys that value. But holding a blocked agent forever blocks capacity.
Solution:
Checkpointing (Temporal + LangGraph checkpointer): persist agent state after each node. On failure or pause, the agent resumes from the last checkpoint, not from scratch. The 50 calls of work are preserved.
Idempotency: a resumed agent must not double-execute side effects (the idempotency-key pattern).
Pause-and-resume, not fail: on sustained 429, put the workflow into a durable wait (Temporal wait_condition / timer) and resume when quota frees. This is the long-horizon analog of "don't kill a running turn."
Cost accounting on resume: track tokens already spent so a resumed agent respects the original budget.
If you get it wrong: Either you lose expensive partial work on every 429 (wasteful), or you pin resources holding blocked agents (starvation). Checkpoint + pause is the middle path.


S6 — The noisy neighbor (one tenant starves the rest)
Problem: One tenant's burst (or a runaway agent) consumes the shared quota; everyone else gets 429s. Or free users starve paid users.
Root cause: Shared quota with no per-tenant isolation. A single large consumer can exhaust a resource that hundreds of small consumers needed.
Solution:
Per-tenant token buckets / weighted fair queuing: each tenant gets a share of the quota proportional to their tier. A burst by one tenant draws down their bucket, not the global pool.
Bulkheads: separate queues/pools per tier (paid vs free, batch vs interactive) so a batch burst can't starve interactive users.
Priority + aging: interactive/paid work preempts batch work; but low priority ages up so it can't starve forever.
If you get it wrong: Your best customer's agent fails because someone else ran a batch job. That's a churn event, not an incident.


S7 — The wrong lever (scaling workers when the provider is the bottleneck)
Problem: Queue depth explodes, so you scale out workers. It doesn't help — and often makes it worse.
Root cause: The bottleneck is the provider quota, not your worker count. More workers = more concurrent LLM calls = more 429s = more retries = more load. You've added fuel to a congestion collapse.
Solution:
Cap concurrency to the provider quota: use a semaphore / concurrency limiter sized to the provider's limits. Workers beyond that cap add nothing.
Autoscale on queue depth, but bounded: scale workers to drain the queue up to the concurrency cap, never beyond it.
Recognize the bottleneck before acting: if 429 rate is high, the fix is demand shaping / quota management / multi-provider — not more workers.
If you get it wrong: You pay for 100 workers that spend their time receiving 429s. This is the most expensive mistake in the whole list.

S8 — The TPM vs RPM confusion
Problem: RPM looks fine (you're under the request limit) but you still get 429s.
Root cause: TPM is the binding constraint for long-context agents. A few 100K-token calls exhaust TPM while RPM stays low. Most monitoring watches RPM and misses TPM.
Solution:
Meter both RPM and TPM per key, and alert on TPM specifically.
Reduce tokens per call: prompt compression, prefix/prompt caching (cached tokens often don't count against TPM the same way), summarize long context, drop stale tool outputs.
Reserve TPM at admission (the reserve/settle pattern): estimate worst-case tokens, hold them, true-up on actual usage.
If you get it wrong: You tune for the wrong quota and keep hitting a wall you're not measuring.

