# A thundering herd against a rate-limited provider is a contention problem, and the first thing I establish is that the bottleneck is the provider quota, not my workers — so scaling out is the wrong lever and can make it worse. I defend in layers. At the door, admission control and leaky-bucket smoothing shape the burst, and I stagger cron so I don't self-inflict it. I treat quota as a first-class resource with a provider-quota scheduler that reserves RPM and TPM per call and converts a would-be 429 into a routing or wait decision. Retries are made safe with full jitter, Retry-After, a per-workflow and global retry budget, and a circuit breaker so I fail fast instead of hammering. Long-horizon work is protected with checkpointing and pause-and-resume so a 429 doesn't destroy fifty calls of sunk work. Fairness is enforced with per-tenant buckets and bulkheads so one tenant can't starve the rest. And I meter TPM specifically, because for long-context agents TPM is the binding constraint. The result: the burst is smoothed, the quota is respected rather than crashed into, retries don't amplify, and partial work is never lost.

The traps they'll throw
"Why not just scale workers?" → "The provider quota is fixed; more workers means more concurrent calls means more 429s. I cap concurrency to the quota and shape demand instead."
"Why not retry harder?" → "Retries are load. A retry storm can exceed the original burst. I budget retries and shed over retrying."
"RPM is fine, why the 429s?" → "TPM is the binding constraint for long-context agents. I meter and reserve TPM, not just RPM."
"What about the agent that's 90% done?" → "Checkpoint + pause-and-resume. I never lose sunk work to a 429, and I never pin resources holding a blocked agent."

The one-liner
"Shape the burst, schedule the quota, budget the retries, protect the sunk work — and never scale into a fixed quota."



